      "text": "We start by setting up an inequality to represent the relationship between the square of the integer and its double:\n\\[x^2 < 2x\\]\nNext, we rearrange the inequality to isolate the variable:\n\\[x^2 - 2x < 0\\]\nFactoring the quadratic expression, we get:\n\\[x(x - 2) < 0\\]\nTo determine the values of \\(x\\) that satisfy this inequality, we can use a sign chart:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nx & -\\infty & 0 & 2 & \\infty \\\\\n\\hline\nx(x - 2) & \\text{Positive} & 0 & \\text{Negative} & \\text{Positive} \\\\\n\\hline\nx(x - 2) < 0 & \\text{No solution} & 0 & \\text{No solution} & \\text{Solution} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nFrom the sign chart, we can see that the inequality \\(x(x - 2) < 0\\) is satisfied when \\(0 < x < 2\\).\nSince we are looking for the only integer solution, the only integer that satisfies this inequality is \\(x = 1\\).\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
